Compare optimized closures on generated theories

Exercise all four combinations of naive/semi-naive evaluation and
native/desugared morphisms against the same reproducible inputs. Anchor
isomorphism comparisons to supplied inputs after every closure so a
renaming cannot hide differences in their consequences.

Exercise authored scenarios through Rust tests using generated setters
and explicit assertions. Cover joins through merged witnesses, diamonds
of morphisms, equal models and parallel morphisms, and nested images.
Randomize morphism graphs, partial and non-injective maps, and edits
between closures to expose propagation and equality interactions.

Also generate finite, typed random theories and independent seeded edit
sequences. Preserve each variant's evaluation partitions between rounds
and save failing random theories and symbolic traces for replay.

Dispatch runtime-typed edits to generated setters in place, after
validating on a snapshot. Keep compiled instances alive across the
whole operation trace so importing all facts as new cannot mask
incremental evaluation bugs.

Provide checked model homomorphisms and isomorphism witnesses on
equality classes so evaluation strategies can be compared despite
different element allocation and index layouts. Support comparison
under a common base even when its elements become identified during
evaluation.

Expose canonicalization on dynamic and compiled models so equality
updates can be applied without running rules or losing pending work.
Preserve existing handles and old/new partitions, promoting rewritten
old facts to new. Refresh generated query indices and shared views.

Require canonicalized inputs for isomorphism search. Use joint color
refinement and backtracking to find type-preserving bijections that
preserve and reflect all relations, including function graphs and
membership. Keep homomorphisms tied to immutable model borrows so
their validation cannot become stale.

Accept explicit finite or infinite fuel for both isomorphism searches
so callers can bound expensive comparisons. Account for input indices,
refinement, and backtracking work by data size, and report exhaustion
separately from a completed search that finds no isomorphism.
